Single arm, 281 lines, clippy 0.

Probed all nine behaviours. The pair that carries it, same stdout both times:

  exited_zero=true   Ok(VerifyOutput { correct: false, detail: "mismatch at 3" })
  exited_zero=false  Err(Failed("{\"correct\":false,...}"))

An incorrect kernel is a MEASUREMENT and reads Ok; a crashed process is not a
measurement and reads Err, even though its stdout contained valid JSON. Collapsing
those is how a benchmark that died would score as a correct slow kernel.

The rest:
  ms 12.5           Ok(12.5)
  log lines then    Ok(7.25)        progress on stdout does not make a run unreadable
  ms 0.0            Ok(0.0)         a zero measurement is a measurement
  ms -1             Err(NotAMeasurement)   a negative millisecond is not a fast kernel
  two objects       Ok(2.0)         last one wins, as pinned
  empty stdout      Err(Unparseable)
  {"ms":"fast"}     Err(Unparseable)

